<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements (spec §52.9).

  Tauri 2 sets `bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime` to TRUE by default, so any build
  that is actually signed runs under the hardened runtime. Under the hardened
  runtime the microphone is gated by an entitlement, NOT just by the
  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription string in Info.plist — with the usage string but
  no entitlement the prompt never appears and the mic is silently dead. That is
  the "works in dev, mic dead in the signed DMG" failure this file prevents:
  `tauri dev` runs unsigned and unrestricted, so the gap only shows up in the
  shipped artifact.

  Referenced from tauri.conf.json as `bundle.macOS.entitlements`. That IS the
  supported field in Tauri 2 — unlike @electron/osx-sign, where a top-level
  `entitlements` option is silently ignored and must go through
  optionsForFile. Signing itself stays opt-in: `signingIdentity` is left unset
  so it is driven by the APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY environment variable, and an
  unsigned local build simply never consults this file.

  Deliberately NOT included: `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` and
  `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`. Electron needs those for V8; Speaking Glass
  renders in WKWebView, whose JIT lives in a separate system process, so
  granting them here would only weaken the runtime for nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Voice tracking and the microphone test (spec §12, §14.2 D). -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Outbound HTTPS for the speech-model download offered by the Welcome
       screen and Settings › Speech, which is the only network activity the
       shipped app has (§152, docs/network-inventory.md).
       Transcription itself is on-device and
       needs no network at all — this key predates that change and used to name
       a hosted STT provider, which no code has talked to since. Strictly it is
       an App Sandbox key and is inert under the hardened runtime alone; it is
       declared so that enabling the sandbox later does not silently kill the
       model download. Keep this comment true: it is a claim about the network
       boundary, and legal/PRIVACY_POLICY.md and .github/SECURITY.md make the
       same claim to users. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
